    "basis": "published-pre-1930",
    "detail": "Published 1867 as 'Nobody Knows the Trouble I've Had' in Slave Songs of the United States, ed. Allen, Ware and Garrison (A. Simpson and Co., New York); long out of copyright in the United States.",
    "sourceEdition": "Traditional melody in F major, Frank Nordberg transcription of the anonymous spiritual (musicaviva USA collection), checked against the 1867 Slave Songs reading",
    "notes": "Melody and changes only; no lyrics are carried. This is the traditional tune, not the Burleigh concert setting or any later recorded arrangement. Chords are a bar-granular reduction; the printed chart moves IV mid-bar in three places and v1 folds each bar into its primary chord."
  },
  "key": "F",
  "mode": "major",
  "meter": "4/4",
  "tempoBPM": 68,
  "tempoRange": [50, 96],
  "feel": "straight",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "notes": "Common time throughout, no meter reduction needed. Confidence is medium: the arc of the tune and the held notes are certain, but folk transcriptions differ on a couple of passing notes in the second half of each line. The refrain carries the tune's one-beat pickup (the tonic) on its last beat so refrain into verse keeps the upbeat; the verse starts on the downbeat.",
